  <!-- § 11 what this measures: evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The ILO is the United Nations agency for work. Its statistical database, ILOSTAT, collects labour statistics from national agencies, reprocesses survey files where it can get them, and publishes everything on the standards agreed at the International Conference of Labour Statisticians (ICLS). Indian data reaches ILOSTAT from MoSPI's surveys and from the Labour Bureau and other offices.</p>
      <p>The modelled estimates follow the thirteenth ICLS definition of work, which counts producing goods for the family's own use, such as grain grown for the household. The newer nineteenth ICLS definition does not; the ILO's survey series name the definition they use. Most of the ILO's India survey series are on the older definition.</p>
      <p>Each ILO edition re-estimates the whole history: the 2010 figure in the November 2025 edition may differ from the 2010 figure in the 2024 edition. This guide keeps the edition with the data and archives the previous one when a new edition lands. The ILO publishes about 2.5 million figures for India across 693 tables; this guide carries every modelled series and every survey table cut by sex and one other characteristic, and lists the rest with links in the coverage ledger.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 12 misreadings -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">12</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"The ILO says India's unemployment was steady through the 2000s."</h3><p>For those years the ILO's model did not take an Indian survey figure as its own; it imputed one and drew a flat line inside a wide band. Only from {T.urFirst} does the line follow measured data.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"The ILO and MoSPI disagree, so one of them is wrong."</h3><p>They ask different questions of the same survey: work last week against work over the year, calendar years against July to June. Compare like with like, and never join one to the other.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"Women's participation has risen, so women have found jobs."</h3><p>Much of the rise is women counted in unpaid family farm and household work. That is real work, but not the salaried job the phrase suggests.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"India ranks below Bangladesh on women at work."</h3><p>The ILO itself warns that imputed estimates should not be used to rank countries. The comparison shows a gap on common definitions; it is not a league table.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"The ILO's projection for 2027 tells us what will happen."</h3><p>It tells us what the model expects if recent trends hold. No survey stands behind it.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

